      "text": "Given the equation of the line $2x + 3y = 6$, we can express this in the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$ to find its slope $m$. \n\nStarting from $2x + 3y = 6$, we solve for $y$:\n\n\\[3y = -2x + 6\\]\n\\[y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\\]\n\nFrom this equation, we see that the slope of the given line is $m = -\\frac{2}{3}$. The slopes of two perpendicular lines are found to be negative reciprocals of each other. Thus, the slope of a line perpendicular to this one is the negative reciprocal of $-\\frac{2}{3}$, which is:\n\n\\[-\\left(\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\right) = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).",
