      "text": "To find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we first need to determine the slope of the given line. The given line is in the standard form \\(Ax + By = C\\), and its slope can be found by rewriting it in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope.\n\nStarting with the equation \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we isolate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n3y = -2x + 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\]\nFrom this equation, we see that the slope \\(m\\) of the given line is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\).\n\nThe slope of a line that is perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line. Therefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the given line is:\n\\[\n-\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).",
